#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:182
msgid "`fetchone()` - the next row, or `None` at the end;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:183
msgid "`fetchall()` - all (remaining) rows as a list;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:184
msgid "`fetchmany(n)` - the next `n` rows (for big results - see the Self-study part)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:186
msgid "`con.execute(...)` is a shortcut of SQLite: it creates the cursor for you and returns it. With other drivers, use `con.cursor()` first."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:189
msgid "Loop over the rows"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:191
msgid "A cursor is iterable - loop over it like over a file ([session 2](./02-conditions-and-loops.md#the-rows-of-a-file)): one row at a time, little memory:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:205
msgid "The values come back **typed**: `outstanding` is a `float`, `stage` an `int` - no `float(row[7])` as with a CSV file."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:208
msgid "Advanced: `fetchmany` and a second query on the same cursor"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:209
msgid "`fetchmany(n)` takes the next `n` rows; a new `execute` on the same cursor starts a new result:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:225
msgid "A mistake in the SQL is an exception like any other ([lecture 8](./08-errors-and-files.md#_1-errors-and-exceptions)) - in SQLite an `OperationalError`, with the message of the database:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:239
msgid "Advanced: rows into dataclasses"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:240
msgid "Rows straight into the dataclasses of [lecture 10](./10-oop-classes.md#_7-dataclasses) - `*row` unpacks the tuple into the fields:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:263
msgid "The column names"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:265
msgid "`cursor.description` describes the columns of the last query; its first item per column is the name:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:280
msgid "Or let SQLite give each row **by name** - `row_factory = sqlite3.Row`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:294
msgid "Close the connection - always"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:296
msgid "A connection holds resources on the database server (and a lock on an SQLite file). Close it also when the query fails: `try` / `finally` ([lecture 8](./08-errors-and-files.md#_3-else-and-finally)), or `contextlib.closing` in a `with`:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:309
msgid "`with sqlite3.connect(...)` does not close"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:310
msgid "For `sqlite3`, `with con:` is a **transaction** (section 4), not a close. Use `closing(...)` - or `con.close()` in `finally` - to close. The short examples below write `with sqlite3.connect(...) as con:` to save a line: the connection is closed when the example ends. In a script that runs for a long time, close it yourself."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:316
msgid "Types in SQLite"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:318
msgid "SQLite has few types: `INTEGER`, `REAL`, `TEXT`, `BLOB` and `NULL`. **Dates are text** (ISO, `2026-09-30`) - they sort and compare correctly as text ([session 2](./02-conditions-and-loops.md#strings-are-compared-letter-by-letter)), and you convert them in Python when you need dates:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:332
msgid "(`start, = ...` unpacks a tuple of one value.) `NULL` comes back as `None`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:342
msgid "Write a query for the 5 clients with the most loans (`GROUP BY client_id`), and print them with `row_factory = sqlite3.Row` as `C0030: 5 loans`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:346
msgid "3. Parameterised queries"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:348
msgid "`?` - the value goes separately"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:350
msgid "A query with a value from your program - a stage, a region, a report date. Write a `?` in the SQL, and pass the values as a **tuple**, separately:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:365
msgid "A one-value tuple needs the comma: `(3,)`. With many parameters, named ones are easier to read - `:name` and a dictionary:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:382
msgid "(Other drivers use other placeholders: `%s` for psycopg and pyodbc's `?`, `:name` for oracledb. The idea is the same.)"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:385
msgid "Advanced: dates and `LIKE` patterns as parameters"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:386
msgid "Dates go as ISO text into SQLite (other drivers accept `date` objects directly), and `LIKE` patterns are values too:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:405
msgid "A query with parameters inside a function is a reusable report - the function of [session 4](./04-functions.md), the SQL of today:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:422
msgid "`(\"C0002\")` is not a tuple"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:423
msgid "Without the comma, the brackets do nothing: the parameter is the **string**, and a string is a sequence of 5 characters - 5 values for one `?`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:436
msgid "Why never an f-string: SQL injection"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:438
msgid "It is tempting to build the SQL text with an f-string. Do not - with values from outside (a file, a form, a parameter of a script), the text can **change the query**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:454
msgid "With a parameter, the driver sends the value **as a value** - it can never become SQL. Parameters also handle quotes in names (`O'Neil`), dates and numbers correctly, and let the database reuse the query plan."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:460
msgid "`where region = \"&region\"` (macro text)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:461
msgid "`%superq`, quoting functions"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:461
msgid "not needed: the value is never text of the query"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:463
msgid "Values: parameters. Names: your own code only"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:464
msgid "Parameters work for **values**. Table and column names cannot be parameters - if a script must choose a table, take the name from a fixed list in your code, never directly from an input."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:469
msgid "Advanced: a parameter in the place of a name"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:470
msgid "A parameter in the place of a **name** does not fail - it is worse: `ORDER BY ?` sorts by the constant text `\"outstanding\"`, which is the same for every row, so the order is not what you asked for:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:483
msgid "`IN` lists"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:485
msgid "`WHERE x IN (?)` with a list does not work - one `?` is one value. Make one `?` per item:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:500
msgid "The f-string builds only the **placeholders** - the values still go as parameters. (For thousands of values, `itertools.batched` of [lecture 9](./09-modules-and-git.md) cuts the list into pieces.)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:504
msgid "The same query, many values: `executemany`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-databases.md:516
msgid "`\":memory:\"` creates a database that lives only while the connection is open - for examples and tests."
msgstr ""

